    "body": "Has someone confirmed that this works in a newer Bash version, or is that an assumption? I am hitting the same bug in Ubuntu 20.04's default Bash installation: `GNU bash, version 5.0.17(1)-release (x86_64-pc-linux-gnu)`.\r\n\r\nThe wording \"ridiculously long\" and \"ludicrously long\" implies this doesn't come up in normal usage. In my case, I have a two-line prompt, where the second line is very short, yet I still have the same issue. The first line takes up the entire terminal width, using the `$fill` feature. The second line is simply a rocketship emoji followed by whatever command I input. Here's how it looks right before it should start wrapping:\r\n\r\n```\r\n~/code vscode  master [!?]―――――――――exit 0...❯ 7ms at 21:17:27\r\n🚀: git commit -m \"Here is my commit message that is too long\r\n```\r\n\r\nIf I were to keep typing, it would wrap around to the same line, overwriting the beginning of the line. Like so:\r\n\r\n```\r\n~/code vscode  master [!?]―――――――――exit 0...❯ 7ms at 21:17:27\r\nto fit on one line\"Here is my commit message that is too long \r\n```",
    "author_association": "NONE",
